3.2 NMR and Mass spectrometric studies of the recovered 1 after water oxidation reactions
The digested sample of 1 was also characterized by electrospray ionization-mass spectrometry (ESI-MS). The sample was digested either under acidic condition using 0.1 M $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4},$ /DMSO or under basic condition using $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaOH/H_{2}O}$ , before diluted with $1\%$ formic acid/methanol solution for ESI-MS measurements. For the sample digested with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ , molecular ion peaks for $\mathrm{\small{[(bpy-dc)Ir(H_{2}P O_{4})(H C O_{2})C l]}}$ were observed $\left(\left[\mathrm{M}\mathrm{+N}\mathrm{a}\right]^{+}$ , expected: 789.00, observed: 789.06, Figure 3c). For the sample digested with NaOH, molecular ion peaks for [(bpy$\mathbf{dc})\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})\mathbf{Cl}]^{+}$ were observed $\left(\mathbf{M}^{+}$ , expected: 705.06, observed:705.14, Figure S20 [SI]). The assignments of these species are unambiguous because of the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopic data and the unique isotope patterns of the molecular ions in the mass spectra. We believe that $\mathrm{\small{[(bpy-dc)Ir(H_{2}O)_{2}(H C O_{2})C l]}^{+}}$ is the most likely precatalyst in 1-catalyzed WORs (Scheme 2). Presumably, $\mathrm{\small{[(bpy-dc)Ir(H_{2}O)_{2}(H C O_{2})C l]}^{+}}$ is oxidized by $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ via a proton-coupled electron transfer process to form the corresponding $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ir}}(\mathrm{V})=0$ species (Scheme 2) which initiates the water oxidation catalytic cycle.
Although significant catalyst modification was observed for the MOF-catalyzed WORs, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation curves did not show initial induction periods. This observation can be explained by the gradual modification of the $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ ligand to the $\left[(\mathbf{bpy-dc})\mathrm{Ir(H_{2}O)_{2}X C l}\right]^{+}$ species, which was supported by the slow increase of acetic acid upon the addition of $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ . The lack of an initial induction period in the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation curve further implies that the original $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ ligand in the MOF is also an active water oxidation with a comparable activity to that of the oxidatively modified one (i.e., the $\left[(\mathbf{bpy-dc})\mathrm{Ir(H_{2}O)_{2}X C l}\right]^{+}$ species). Our experimental data however cannot unambiguously confirm this hypothesis.
## 3.3 Water oxidation activity of soluble (bpy)Ir(solvent) $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{n}}X_{\mathbf{m}}$ species
To support the hypothesis that (bpy-dc) $\mathrm{Ir(H_{2}O)_{2}X C l}$ is a true precatalyst for 1-catalyzed WORs, we synthesized (bpy)Ir complexes containing water and chloride groups but no other ancillary ligands to test their water oxidation activities. Although $\mathrm{{K[{Ir(bpy)Cl_{4}}]}}$ was not a competent WOC, its derivatives after treatment with ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ showed water oxidation activities with ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ as the oxidant. Upon treating $\mathrm{{K[{Ir(bpy)Cl_{4}}]}}$ with four equivalents of ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ in $\mathrm{pH}=1$ (with added ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}{\mathrm{.}}$ ), the resultant soluble species showed complicated $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR spectra, indicating the formation of multiple species due to incomplete Cl removal. Nevertheless, the resulting $\mathrm{(bpy)Ir(solvent)_{n}X_{m}}$ mixture (with water and chloride or nitrate groups in addition to the bpy ligand, $25~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ based on Ir) was highly active for water oxidation in the presence of 10 $\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ at $\mathsf{p H}=1$ . However, the water oxidation activity of this mixture decreased with time with an intractable dark blue solid forming within one hour (Figure S21 [SI]). The solid formation was due to aggregation of the $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{IV})$ or $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{V})$ molecular species; such a process was prevented in the MOFs as a result of catalyst site isolation in the frameworks.
