3.5 XPS analyses of 1 and 2 before and after water oxidation reactions.
XPS analysis was performed on the recovered MOF solids ( $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ based on Ir) after treatment with various $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ ’s (0.3, 3, and $200\mathrm{mM},$ at $\mathrm{pH}{=}1$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . All the MOF samples were washed with nitric acid and dried on silicon wafer and then kept in vacuum overnight prior to XPS measurements. The spectra were calibrated with respect to the C(1s) peaks in each sample. The $\mathrm{Ir}(4\mathrm{f})$ region of the spectra showed no difference in peak positions between the original MOF and the samples treated with different $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]^{3}\mathrm{s}$ , indicating the Ir(III) oxidation state in all of these samples (Figure S23 [SI]). We noticed that the MOFs remained in green color in a $\mathrm{\pH=1}$ solution after all of the $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ ions were consumed. The green color suggested the Ir(IV) oxidation state for the resting state of the catalysts at low pH. These MOFs however reverted to the original yellowred color after treatment with neutral water (see Section 3.6). We believe that the observation of the Ir(III) oxidation state by XPS is due to the ready reduction of the recovered MOFs either during the drying and vacuum processing prior to XPS measurements or by X-rays or the generated photoelectrons during the XPS measurements. XPS thus cannot be considered a reliable technique for determining the oxidation states of Ir WOCs. The signal intensity of Ir(4f), N(1s), and $Z\mathrm{r}(3\mathrm{d})$ peaks all decreased with respect to the C(1s) peak intensity at increasing $\lbrack\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ ’s, presumably caused by adsorbed $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ ions on the MOF surface. The XPS spectra also showed two different N(1s) peaks at binding energies of 398.2 and $404.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the pyridyl and nitrate nitrogen atoms, respectively (Figure 5). The atomic ratios of Ir vs. pyridyl nitrogen in these samples provide useful information on the stability of chelating bpy-dc and ppy-dc ligands. As shown in Table S2 ([SI]), the atomic ratios of Ir vs. pyridyl nitrogen remained constant for both 1 and 2 after treatment with relatively low $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ ’s, suggesting that the Ir atom and pyridine moiety in bpy-dc and ppy-dc ligands remain intact under these conditions. However, under high $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ ’s, the atomic ratios of Ir vs. pyridyl nitrogen decreased significantly compared to those of the original MOFs, suggesting loss of Ir from the MOFs as a result of partial degradation of the chelating ligands (see Section 4). It is noted that the deviation of Ir vs. pyridyl nitrogen atomic ratios from the ideal atomic ratios is due to different electron collection efficiency for different elements in the samples, which is not uncommon for XPS analyses.
3.6 Diffuse-reflectance UV-Vis measurements of 1 after water oxidation reactions.
The recovered 1 after treatment with ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ $(3\mathrm{\mM})$ overnight was washed with copious amounts of $0.1\mathrm{~M~HNO}_{3}$ solution. The recovered solid exhibits a green color, suggesting an Ir(IV) species as the catalyst resting state. Note that if the solid is washed with water $\mathrm{(pH=7)}$ ), the green color will slowly change to the original yellow-red color, suggesting that the $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{IV})$ species is not stable at neutral pH. Diffuse-reflectance UV-Vis spectrum of the recovered 1 (after washing with 0.1 M ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solution) was taken by pressing the solids onto a smooth $\mathrm{BaSO_{4}}$ surface. As shown in Figure 6a, an additional peak at ${\sim}600\ \mathrm{nm}$ appears in the solid when compared to 1, corresponding to the $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{IV})$ species at resting state.9 Interestingly, the Ir(IV) species of the recovered 1 was readily reduced to $\operatorname{Ir}(\operatorname{III})$ species by adding a few drops of ethanol to the pressed solid sample (for diffuse reflectance UV-Vis measurements). The spectrum taken after evaporation of ethanol lost the peak at ${\sim}600~\mathrm{nm}$ , suggesting a complete reduction of the Ir(IV) species to Ir(III). This reversible oxidation/reduction of the Ir species is consistent with the molecular nature of water oxidation catalysts. The peak intensities of the diffuse-reflectance spectra cannot be exactly reproduced in each measurement due to different sample positions and other experimental variables.
