4. $\scriptstyle{[\mathbf{Ce}^{4+}]}$ -dependent degradation of MOF water oxidation catalysts.
The observed degradation of 2 at $3\mathrm{\mM}$ of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ prompted us to carefully examine the degradation of MOF WOCs during WORs. The degradation of MOF WOCs can be inferred from the kinetic data of WORs as determined by ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ consumption rates using UV-Vis spectroscopy. The catalyst decomposition rate is shown to be very sensitive to the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ .
For 1-catalyzed reactions, the kinetics of $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ consumption becomes much faster when the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ exceeds $4.5~\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure 4a), suggesting the decomposition of the MOF catalyst to form more active WOCs under these conditions. This decomposition kinetics can be more clearly visualized by plotting $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]/\mathrm{C}_{0}$ vs. $t$ ( $\mathrm{\tilde{C}}_{0}$ is the initial concentration of $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ ) (Figures S28 and S29 [SI]). At $\mathrm{C}_{0}{<}4.5\mathrm{mM}$ , $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]/\mathrm{C}_{0}$ decays more slowly as $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ becomes higher. However, At $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ $>4.5\mathrm{mM}$ , $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]/\mathrm{C}_{0}$ decays faster as $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ becomes higher. This $\lbrack\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ -dependent behavior indicates that MOF decomposition has a high order dependence on $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ .
To further confirm this $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ -dependent decomposition, the MOF after being treated with 9 $\mathrm{mM}~\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ was recovered after all of the $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ ions were consumed and reused in a new run of water oxidation with $3{\mathrm{~mM~Ce}}^{4+}$ . The recovered MOF showed much faster $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ consumption as shown in Figure 4c, supporting the decomposition of the MOF at high $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ ’s. To test if the decomposition is caused by the generated $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ , a solution with $1.0\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ and $9{\mathrm{mM}}\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ was used in a catalytic run. The added $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ slowed the ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ consumption rate, ruling out the possibility that $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ caused decomposition of the MOF (Figure 7d). The slower ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ consumption of the $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ -added solution is due to slower $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ diffusion at a higher total Ce concentration, which will be further discussed in Section 5.
UV-Vis data showed that 2 is more prone to decomposition than 1. At an initial $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ of 3 mM, 2 quickly decomposed as indicated by the ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ consumption rate. The decomposition of 2 is further supported by the observation that at an initial $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ of $0.5\mathrm{~-~}3\mathrm{~}\mathrm{mM}$ , the ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ consumption rate was much higher for the second catalytic run than the first one (Figure. S50- S55 [SI]).
MOF decomposition was also confirmed by PXRD studies. PXRD patterns of the MOFs were taken at different time points with different initial $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ ’s. The broadening or even disappearance of the PXRD peaks at high $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ ’s is indicative of framework decomposition. Moreover, there is strong correlation between UV-Vis kinetics and PXRD patterns on the onset of MOF decomposition. At higher $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ ’s, the UV-Vis data showed accelerated consumption of $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ when the PXRD pattern indicated that MOFs started to decompose. It is likely that the Ir moieties are released into solution to form much more active non-molecular WOCs when the MOF structures collapse, leading to much enhanced ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ consumption rates.
To investigate if the MOF decomposition is due to the instability of the SBUs, we synthesized an analogous UiO MOF using the 5-benzoic-2-(4-benzoic-phenyl)-pyridine acid (ppy-dc) bridging ligand and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ SBUs. This Ir-free MOF exhibits the same UiO topology but adopts an interpenetrated structure as revealed by the systematic absence of the [200] peak in its PXRD pattern (Figure S22 [SI]). As shown by the PXRD patterns of the MOF after acid and $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ treatment, the MOF is stable in $\mathsf{p H}{=}1$ water solution and is much more stable than 1 and 2 at high $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ . This control experiment indicates that the UiO framework and SBUs are stable under WOR conditions. The decomposition observed for 1 and 2 at high $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ ’s is thus attributed to degradation of $\mathbf{L_{1}}$ and $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ moieties under these harsh conditions.
## 5. A diffusion-reaction model for MOF-catalyzed water oxidation
Little is known about kinetics of MOF-catalyzed reactions, due to the difficulty in following MOF-catalyzed reactions and complicated data analysis involving both reaction kinetics and reactant/product diffusion rates. In this $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ -driven WOR, however, the reaction can be readily followed by UV-Vis spectroscopy to accurately determine the consumption rate of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ . Kinetic analyses of MOF-catalyzed $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ -driven WORs are further simplified due to two factors: 1) water is the reactant whose concentration remains constant throughout the WOR; 2) the product, oxygen molecules, can diffuse quickly and does not require consideration.
Water oxidation experiments were performed at several different initial $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]`:$ s. The $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ ’s determined from UV-Vis measurements were plotted against reaction time t. 1 and 2 exhibited different kinetic behaviors. For 1, the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]-t$ plot is linear at the initial stage, indicating a quasi-zeroth order reaction with respect to $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ . The deviation from a linear relationship at later times (particularly for higher $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]^{\bullet}\mathrm{s})$ is likely due to the decomposition of MOF catalysts. This quasi-zeroth order reaction rate depends on initial $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ (Figure 8a; Figures S32-S40 [SI]). For 2, the $\ln[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]-t$ plot is linear at the initial stage, indicating a quasi-first order reaction with respect to $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ (Figure 8b; Figures S44-S49 [SI]). We will show below that these seemingly different kinetic behaviors can be explained using the same diffusion-reaction model with different ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivities and WOR rates for 1 and 2.
With $c(r,t)$ denoting the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ inside a MOF (where $r$ is the distance from the center of a spherical MOF particle and $t$ is the reaction time), we can write Eq. 1 to express $c(r,t)$ by considering diffusion of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ inside a MOF particle following Fick’s law (the first term) and the WOR catalyzed by the isolated Ir sites with a first-order dependence on the local $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ (the second term). The corresponding boundary conditions to describe this diffusion-reaction process inside the MOF particle are shown in Eq 2 and Eq 3.
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\frac{\partial c(r,t)}{\partial t}=D\frac{1}{r^{2}}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\Big(r^{2}\frac{\partial c(r,t)}{\partial r}\Big)-k c(r,t)}\\ &{t=0\quad\ 0<r<a,\ c=0;r=a,c=c_{0}}\\ &{r=a,\quad V\frac{\partial c(r,t)}{\partial t}=\ -N\cdot4\pi a^{2}D\frac{\partial c(r,t)}{\partial r}}\end{array}
$$
where the radius of the MOF particle is $a$ (the particle is approximated as spherical).
The total solution volume – V = 2 mL
The MOF concentration (the concentration of ligand $\mathbf{L}$ in the solution) -- $C_{\mathrm{MOF}}=10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ The Avogadro’s constant – $\mathbf{\cdotN_{A}}{=}6.02{\times}10^{23}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$
The volume of unit cell per ligand $\mathbf{L}$ from the crystal structure – $\cdot\nu=2.4\mathrm{nm}^{3}$   
The number of MOF particles in the solution $-\mathbf{N}=V{\cdot}C_{\mathrm{MOF}}{\cdot}\mathbf{N}_{\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}\nu/(4/3\pi a^{3})$   
The diffusivity of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ inside MOF – D   
The initial $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ in solution— $C_{0}$   
The rate constant of reaction inside $\mathrm{MOF}-k$ (first order with respect to $\lbrack\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}])$
Although ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivity should have a strong dependence on ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ and $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ concentrations, a constant diffusivity $D$ is assumed in the model based on two approximations: (1) the diffusion of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ or $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ in the MOF channel is fast compared to the experimental time scale so that the sum of $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]+[\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}]$ within the MOF, especially in the outer-shell that can be reached by ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ (see below), quickly reaches the same level of the bulk solution at the very beginning of the experimental measurement; (2) $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ and $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ , due to the similarity of their chemical structures, have a similar concentration effect on the diffusivity of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ , so that the ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivity only depends on the sum of $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]+[\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}],$ which is constant throughout the course of reaction.
To validate the second approximation of a similar influence of $[\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ on the diffusivity of $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ , we carried out WORs with 1 by adding a mixture of $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ to the catalyst. As shown in Figure 9a and Figure S43 ([SI]), with different initial ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ concentrations $\left(\mathrm{C}_{0}\right)$ but the same total Ce concentration $([\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}]{+}[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}])$ , the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]/\mathrm{C}_{0}$ curves nearly overlap with each other, indicating that ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ exhibits similar diffusivity in 1 under these conditions. Moreover, the kinetic data with different initial $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ concentrations were fitted to the diffusion-reaction model to obtain $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ diffusivities. As shown in Figure S56 ([SI]), The dependence of $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ diffusivities on the sums of $[\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}]+[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ only slightly deviates from the dependence of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivities on the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ when pure ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ was added to the catalyst at the beginning. These results indicate that there is a only slight difference of the dependence of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivity on $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}]$ , thus validating our approximation of a similar influence of $[\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ on the diffusivity of $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ . ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ can be transported into the MOF interior via the $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ self-exchange in addition to the direct diffusion of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ through the channels. However, the negligible dependence of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivity on $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ ratios indicates that the self-exchange pathway is not significant in these reactions.
