Assuming the $[\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}]$ in the bulk solution $C_{s}$ (which can be detected by UV-Vis measurements) equals the $[\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}]$ on the MOF surface, Eq 4 was then obtained, which describes the quasi-first order kinetic behavior for the 2-catalyzed reaction. The amount of $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ inside the MOF at a given time was insignificant $(<0.0043\%)$ compared to the amount in the solution and can be ignored in the $C_{s}$ quantification.
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\frac{d l n(\frac{c_{s}}{c_{0}})}{d t}=-\beta\frac{\sqrt{k D}}{a}(1-\frac{1}{a}\sqrt{\frac{D}{k}})}\end{array}
$$
Here $\upbeta$ is defined as $\frac{N\cdot4\pi a^{3}}{V}$ . For 1, however, a further linearization approximation can be made (Eq S30 [SI]), leading to a quasi-zeroth order reaction described by:
$$
\frac{d\frac{c_{s}}{c_{0}}}{d t}=-\beta\frac{\sqrt{k D}}{a}(1-\frac{1}{a}\sqrt{\frac{D}{k}})
$$
This linearization approximation will only hold when $\begin{array}{r}{\beta\frac{\sqrt{k D}}{a}\bigg(1-\frac{1}{a}\sqrt{\frac{D}{k}}\bigg)t\ll1}\end{array}$ . The validity of this assumption for 1 but not for 2 can be verified by calculating a characteristic time $t_{a}=$ $\scriptstyle\left.{\int}\beta{\frac{\sqrt{k D}}{a}}{\Bigg(}1-{\frac{1}{a}}{\sqrt{\frac{D}{k}}}{\Bigg)}\right\rangle$ Only when $t_{a}\gg t$ , the approximation holds. As shown in Table S4 and
Table S5 [SI], $t_{a}\gg t$ holds for 1 (120 – 670 minutes) but does not hold for 2 (12 – 55 minutes).   
This difference in $t_{a}$ explains different kinetic behaviors of the two MOFs.
By fitting the experimental data to these equations, the $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ diffusivity values were obtained (Table 2). As expected, the ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivities determined from these analyses are highly dependent on the Ce concentration: the lower the concentration, the higher the diffusivity. ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ diffusivities in 2 are significantly larger than those in 1, consistent with the expectation that positively charged species should diffuse faster through neutral channels in 2 than through positively charged channels in 1.
As the experimental time scale is significantly larger than the characteristic diffusional time $\begin{array}{r}{t_{D}=\frac{a^{2}}{D}}\end{array}$ , the diffusion-reaction process inside these MOFs are at the steady-state. With the kinetic model, the ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ distributions inside the MOFs at the steady-state can be calculated, using the following equation (Eq S33-S35 [SI]).
$$
\frac{c}{c_{s}}=\frac{a}{r}\cdot\frac{\sinh(\sqrt{\frac{k}{D}}r)}{\sinh(\sqrt{\frac{k}{D}}a)}
$$
